const body = `<h1>5.1 — The Axe: Item Pickups &amp; Inventory Bools</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Coins and potions changed <em>numbers</em>. The axe changes what you <em>are</em>. It flips a
    boolean — that half is easy, and you have written the pattern three times already — and then
    it replaces the entire machine that draws your character. Nothing you have built so far
    prepares you for the second half, and it is one line long.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I add an item to my inventory?', 2)}

${renderDecompose('5.1', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: A Pickup That Changes You</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every pickup in this game so far has been easy to hold in your head, because it changed a
        number that already existed. Walk into a coin and <code>coins</code> goes up by one. Walk
        into a potion and <code>health</code> goes up. The axe does not do that. Walk into an axe
        and <strong>every number in your game stays exactly where it was</strong> — and the
        character you are controlling becomes somebody else.
    </p>

    <p>
        So answer the data question before you build anything. Coins needed a counter. A potion
        needed to change an existing number. What does "the player is carrying an axe" actually
        need?
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Option</th><th>What it stores</th><th>Why you would or would not</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td>An <code>int</code> count</td>
            <td>How many axes you hold</td>
            <td>Useless extra information. Nobody has ever needed two axes, so you would be storing a number you will never read.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>A <code>bool</code></td>
            <td>Yes or no — you have one, or you do not</td>
            <td>The honest shape of the fact. One item, one yes/no, and the game only ever asks one question about it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>A list of items</td>
            <td>Every item you carry, by name</td>
            <td>The right answer for sixty items. Overkill for one, and it turns the simplest question in the game — "do I have an axe?" — into a search.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The boolean is the easy half, and it is the half that feels like the lesson.</strong>
        Everything this game has remembered so far has been a <em>number that changes</em> — a coin
        count, health, and the moment on the clock you kept on the turret. A <code>bool</code> is
        that same shape wearing different clothes: a value that can only ever be <code>0</code> or
        <code>1</code> is still a value, and you have declared one before. The half that has no
        precedent is what the axe does <em>after</em> the boolean flips: it decides which
        animation set the player is drawn with. Watch for it, because if you treat this lesson as
        "a pickup with a different tag", you will build half of it and not notice.
    </div>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Go and look before you write a line.</strong> Open
        <code>Assets/Prefabs/AxeItem</code> and read the Inspector: a Transform, a Sprite Renderer,
        and the tag <code>Axe</code>. Now notice what is missing — and then count the axes placed
        in your scenes, because the answer is <strong>zero</strong>. The mechanic does not exist
        in the world yet; you are going to build it and then go and put it somewhere.
        <br><br>
        Then select the <strong>Sprite</strong> child under the Player and read the Animator
        component. It is playing <code>Player_Shovel.controller</code> — the state machine
        <a href="1.6.html">1.6</a> taught you to drive. Now open
        <code>Assets/Artwork/Animations/Top_Down/PlayerAnimator/</code> and count what is in
        there: <strong>two complete controllers</strong>, a shovel and an axe, and only one of
        them is referenced by anything in this project. Somebody built you the whole weapon set
        and left the second one on the shelf.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Right-sizing the data</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is a <code>bool</code> the right abstraction for "I have an axe" but the wrong one for "how many coins do I have"?</li>
            <li>At roughly how many items does a collection of booleans become the wrong design? What breaks first — readability, or the number of branches you have to write?</li>
            <li>Where should this boolean live — on the axe, on the player, or somewhere global? Use the reasoning you applied in <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>, then check it against what you actually do below.</li>
            <li>You already store coins and health on <code>PlayerController</code>. Is "has an axe" the same kind of fact? What makes it different from a number, if anything?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: The API for Changing What You Are</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Six of the seven rows below are things you have already typed somewhere in this course, and
        the seventh is the one you are going to look up rather than guess. Read the cost column as
        carefully as the middle one — for the first time in this unit, the cost of the interesting
        row is <em>what it does to the rest of the game</em>, not what it does to a number.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>bool</code></td>
            <td>True or false, one bit of information — the type you first met as <code>isMoving</code> in <a href="1.6.html">1.6</a>, now holding a fact about the player instead of a fact about the input.</td>
            <td>There is nothing in between. It cannot say how many and it cannot say how long — an <code>int</code> counts, a list holds many, and a <code>bool</code> is only ever the two answers. That is a feature exactly once per fact.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField] private bool hasAxe = false;</code></td>
            <td>A class-body field that survives every frame and appears in the Inspector, so you can watch it flip while the game runs — the declaration form from <a href="1.3.html">1.3</a> and the coin count from <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>.</td>
            <td>The <em>starting value</em> is a design decision, not a formality. <code>true</code> and the capability is granted before the player has done anything; <code>false</code> and it is earned. The default is the difference between a reward and a decoration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Collider2D</code> ▸ <code>Is Trigger</code> ☑ ON</td>
            <td>The checkbox that changes what an overlap <em>means</em>: a trigger only <strong>reports</strong> that two shapes met, which is what makes <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> fire rather than <code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code>.</td>
            <td>A trigger pushes nothing. On a pickup that is exactly what you want; on the spike in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> it was the bug — "it did not push me back" and "it did not notice me" look identical from the player's seat.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>other.CompareTag("Axe")</code></td>
            <td>Asks the engine whether the collider you just touched carries that tag. The <code>AxeItem</code> prefab already has it, so there is nothing to create and no string to invent.</td>
            <td>Exact spelling against a tag that has to exist in the project, and it is asked of whichever object <em>owns the collider</em> — move the collider down to a child and the answer is false with nothing in the Console to explain it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>animator.SetBool</code> / <code>SetInteger</code> / <code>SetTrigger</code></td>
            <td>The three calls from 1.6 that make a controller <em>play</em> something: <code>IsWalking</code>, <code>WalkDir</code>, <code>Attack</code>.</td>
            <td>They choose between the states of <strong>one</strong> controller. None of them can reach a clip that is not in the machine you are currently playing — which is why a whole new weapon set is not a parameter, and never can be.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>the Animator's own controller property</strong></td>
            <td>The property on the <code>Animator</code> component that holds <em>which controller it is playing right now</em>. Read it and you know which weapon set is live; assign to it and the entire state machine — states, transitions, clips — is replaced in one line. Its type is <code>RuntimeAnimatorController</code>, which is the same type as the Inspector slot you wire. <strong>Find its name on the Animator component page of the Unity scripting reference rather than guessing it</strong> — the Socratic below asks you to, and the walkthrough leaves the line for you to write.</td>
            <td>It replaces <em>everything</em>: nothing you set on the old controller carries over, so the states and parameters you depend on have to exist in the new one (on this project they do — both controllers carry the same three parameters, which is why 1.6's code needs no edits). And nothing about the swap is saved: a Player rebuilt from the prefab comes back with the controller the prefab was wired with. <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> exists partly because of that.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Destroy(Object)</code></td>
            <td>Removes the pickup's GameObject at the end of the frame — the line your coin and your potion already use.</td>
            <td>It is final, and it takes the <em>object</em> and not the capability. Destroying the axe does not un-set <code>hasAxe</code>: if the axe should ever be lost, some other code has to flip the boolean back, in the place where it is lost.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>What the axe does not do.</strong> Notice what is missing from the walkthrough you
        are about to read: no <code>Debug.Log</code> in the pickup, no counter going up, no number
        changing anywhere. Collect the axe and the Console stays silent, because the axe is not
        feedback — it is a <strong>permission</strong>. A fact that future code will read before it
        allows an action. You are not building a score; you are building a capability, and the
        thing that proves it worked is that the character you are controlling looks different.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Three ways to change a weapon</h3>
        <p>All three of these are legitimate. Read the cost column before you commit:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Assign the other controller at runtime</strong></td>
                <td>The Animator component has a property holding the controller it is currently playing. Hand it the axe controller when the axe is picked up and the whole state machine — states, transitions, clips — is replaced instantly.</td>
                <td>Nothing to author, and it works for any weapon set somebody else has already built. You must remember to set it back if the axe is ever lost, and nothing about it survives a scene load unless something puts it back.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Author the axe states into one controller</strong></td>
                <td>Add the axe states and transitions into the controller you already have, and drive them with a new parameter.</td>
                <td>You duplicate an entire state machine by hand, and every future clip has to be added twice. It also means editing an asset somebody else built for you.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Use an Animator Override Controller</strong></td>
                <td>A Unity asset built for exactly this: it takes one base controller and swaps the clips it plays, keeping all the logic in one place.</td>
                <td>One extra asset to create, and one more concept to learn before you can build anything. This is the professional answer, and it is not today's answer.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>Which approach does the project's own art layout clearly <em>intend</em> you to take? What is the evidence?</li>
            <li>Read <code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts/SceneSwitch.cs</code>. Find where it saves and restores something related to the Animator. Which property does it touch, and what does that prove is possible at runtime?</li>
            <li>Which approach would you choose if this game had four weapons instead of two, and why does the answer change?</li>
            <li>What is the name of the property on the Animator component that holds the controller? Find it in the documentation rather than guessing.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Put the Axe in the World</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — prove the boolean is state, not a message.</strong> The walkthrough had you
        print <code>hasAxe</code> every frame and then delete the print. Do it in that order, and
        watch the Console while you do: a value that reads <code>false</code> for as long as you
        walk around and <code>true</code> from the moment you touch the axe is a value that
        remembers. Then delete it — <code>Update()</code> runs sixty times a second, and a print
        that survives into next lesson is noise you will be reading past.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — decide where the axe lives.</strong> There were zero axe instances in any
        scene when you started, and you placed at least one. Now defend the placement like a level
        designer: which route does the player take to reach it, and what do they meet on the way? An
        axe nobody can find is the same as no axe at all, and no compiler will ever tell you.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — decide what the axe <em>means</em>.</strong> The boolean now exists and
        nothing in your game reads it. That is the correct state for this lesson and the wrong state
        for the next one — 5.2 is where a door starts caring. Write down, before you finish, the one
        piece of future code you are most confident will read this field.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for <code>PlayerController.cs</code>:</strong>

        <p><strong>The pickup — getting it into the world:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>The <code>AxeItem</code> prefab carries the tag <code>Axe</code> and nothing else. What is it missing that every other pickup in this game has, and which object does it go on?</li>
            <li>Trigger or solid — and what is your reason? What exactly would you see and hear if you chose the other one?</li>
            <li>You added a collider to an object that has never had one, which means you cannot see what the engine sees. How did you check the shape in the Scene view rather than guessing, and what does it cost the player if the box is twice the size of the art?</li>
            <li>The prefab ships with <strong>zero</strong> instances in any scene. Where did you place yours, and how would a tester find it without being told?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The state — one field, and the whole lesson hangs off it:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What type is the field, and why is that the right amount of information rather than an <code>int</code> or a list? Use the table at the top of the lesson.</li>
            <li>What value does it start at, and what does the player get for free if you pick the other one? Answer in one sentence about their first five seconds of play.</li>
            <li>Where does the field live, and what breaks if you get that wrong? Use the word "frame" in your answer.</li>
            <li>Nothing in your game reads this field yet. Name one thing that will, and say what you expect that line of code to look like.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The swap — the new mechanism:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which property on the Animator holds the controller it is currently playing, and where did you read that rather than guess it?</li>
            <li>What did you have to do in the Inspector before the swap could work at all — and what does the player see if you skip it?</li>
            <li>The three parameters from 1.6 — <code>IsWalking</code>, <code>WalkDir</code>, <code>Attack</code> — exist on <em>both</em> controllers with the same names and the same types. What does that tell you about how much of your 1.6 code had to change when the axe arrived?</li>
            <li>The swap is one assignment and it is not saved anywhere. What puts it back when a scene loads, and why does that code have to exist?</li>
            <li>The axe is one-use: breaking the door in 5.2 consumes it. What has to happen to the controller at that moment, which script should do it, and why must it not be the pickup branch?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and press Play. Walk into the axe and swing — the character should visibly become
        someone else the moment you touch it, and stay that way. Then walk through a cave mouth and
        come back, and check that they still are.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The phantom axe</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set <code>hasAxe</code> to start at the "already has it" state and press Play. What does the game grant you before you ever found the axe — and what did the default you originally chose protect against?</li>
            <li>Pick up the axe, then die using the death rule you built in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>. Do you still have the axe? Should you — and is that a coding decision or a design decision?</li>
            <li>Add a second axe and collect both. What does the boolean become after the second one? Why is that the only correct answer even though you touched two axes?</li>
            <li>What happens to the player's appearance when a <em>scene loads</em>? Walk through a cave mouth with the axe and look. Then find the code in <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> that deals with it, and explain why that code has to exist at all.</li>
            <li>Change the name your code uses for an Animator parameter so it no longer matches the controller. What is the symptom — silence, an error, or nothing at all? What does that tell you about the cost of string-based APIs?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You have a mechanic that works and a design that is still entirely yours. These four
        questions are about the design half — and the first one is a look at what this project
        <em>does not</em> have, which tells you something about how the person who built it expected
        weapons to be added.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Inventory design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Look in <code>Assets/Artwork/Character/Player/ExtraSpriteSheets/</code>. It holds <strong>four</strong> sprite sheets: no weapon, shovel, slingshot, and one that pairs the slingshot with a shield. Now count the controllers in <code>Assets/Artwork/Animations/Top_Down/PlayerAnimator/</code>. There are only <strong>two</strong> — shovel and axe. So the art for a slingshot exists and nobody ever authored its states. Under the approach you chose, what would it cost you to make the slingshot real — and does that change which of the three approaches you would pick for a game with four weapons?</li>
            <li>Design the feedback: how would a player <em>see</em> that they are holding an axe? Argue sprite-only versus a UI icon — and which does the animation swap give you for free?</li>
            <li>The one-use axe: breaking the door consumes it, so the boolean flips back to false. Which script should flip it back, and why must it not be the pickup branch that does so?</li>
            <li>Right now your boolean is lost when the scene reloads. Write down exactly what that means for the player. You will fix this in <a href="5.3.html">5.3</a>, and the fix will not be about the axe.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain when a bool fits a fact and when it does not, and right-size the data for a new pickup</li>
            <li>Build an item that grants capability instead of changing a number — trigger collider, tag check, default value and all</li>
            <li>Compare the three ways to change a weapon set, and defend your choice</li>
            <li>Find a property in the Unity documentation rather than guessing its name</li>
            <li>Explain what <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> does about the Animator, and why it must</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

